{"id":"neuro-pharmacology","system":"Neurology & Special Senses","subject":"Pharmacology","pages":[560,568],"rounds":[{"t":"Anticonvulsants","q":[{"s":"A man on long-term therapy for focal seizures has overgrown gums, coarse facial hair and nystagmus. Which drug is he taking?","o":["Ethosuximide","Levetiracetam","Valproate","Phenytoin","Gabapentin"],"e":["Wrong. Absence only.","Wrong. Psychiatric effects.","Wrong. Hair loss, weight gain.","Correct. Gingival hypertrophy is preventable with folate.","Wrong. Weight gain."],"a":3,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Phenytoin and carbamazepine share which mechanism?","o":["SV2A blockade","Na+ channel blockade","GABA-A potentiation","T-type Ca2+ channel blockade","GABA transaminase inhibition"],"e":["Wrong. Levetiracetam.","Correct.","Wrong. Benzodiazepines.","Wrong. Ethosuximide.","Wrong. Valproate."],"a":1,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"A patient started on carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia develops hyponatremia. Mechanism?","o":["Adrenal insufficiency","Diuretic effect","Mineralocorticoid deficiency","SIADH","Diabetes insipidus"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Hypernatremia."],"a":3,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Which serious hematologic effect requires monitoring of blood counts with carbamazepine?","o":["Agranulocytosis and aplastic anemia","Thrombocytosis","Eosinophilia only","Polycythemia","Hemolytic anemia"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not listed."],"a":0,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Phenytoin and carbamazepine cause drug interactions by:","o":["Inhibiting renal excretion","Inducing CYP450","Displacing drugs from albumin only","Inhibiting CYP450","Blocking P-glycoprotein"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not the listed mechanism.","Wrong. Valproate.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"A 7-year-old with absence seizures only should receive which drug, which blocks T-type Ca2+ channels?","o":["Phenobarbital","Gabapentin","Ethosuximide","Phenytoin","Carbamazepine"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Focal.","Wrong. Can worsen absence."],"a":2,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Gabapentin and pregabalin act by:","o":["Inhibiting GABA transaminase","Increasing Cl− channel opening duration","Blocking Na+ channels","Binding SV2A","Blocking Ca2+ channels"],"e":["Wrong. Valproate.","Wrong. Barbiturates.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Levetiracetam.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Which anticonvulsant has the highest risk of teratogenicity?","o":["Gabapentin","Lamotrigine","Valproate","Levetiracetam","Ethosuximide"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Lower risk.","Correct. Neural tube defects.","Wrong. Lower risk.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"A child on valproate for generalized seizures develops vomiting, jaundice and severe epigastric pain radiating to the back. Which serious effects should be suspected?","o":["Gingival hyperplasia","Agranulocytosis","Kidney stones","SJS and DRESS","Hepatotoxicity and pancreatitis"],"e":["Wrong. Phenytoin.","Wrong. Carbamazepine.","Wrong. Topiramate.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Which mechanisms does valproate use?","o":["GABA-A potentiation only","Na+ and Ca2+ channel blockade and GABA transaminase inhibition","SV2A binding only","NMDA antagonism","Glutamate synthesis blockade"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Levetiracetam.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Valproate differs from phenytoin in its effect on CYP450 because it:","o":["Inhibits CYP450","Induces CYP450","Has no effect","Blocks P-glycoprotein","Activates CYP2D6 only"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Phenytoin.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"A woman starting lamotrigine develops fever, blistering mucosal lesions and skin sloughing. Diagnosis?","o":["Stevens-Johnson syndrome","Urticaria only","Erythema nodosum","Serum sickness","Drug-induced lupus"],"e":["Correct. Also risk of DRESS.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Levetiracetam acts by binding:","o":["Synaptic vesicle protein 2A","NMDA receptors","T-type Ca2+ channels","GABA transaminase","GABA-A receptors"],"e":["Correct. Reduces glutamate release.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Ethosuximide.","Wrong. Valproate.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"A man started on levetiracetam becomes irritable, aggressive and develops psychosis. What is the rare but serious adverse effect?","o":["Pancreatitis","Neuropsychiatric effects","Gingival hyperplasia","Hepatotoxicity","SIADH"],"e":["Wrong. Valproate.","Correct.","Wrong. Phenytoin.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Carbamazepine."],"a":1,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"A patient on topiramate for migraine prophylaxis develops flank pain and hematuria. Diagnosis?","o":["Renal vein thrombosis","Pancreatitis","Kidney stones","Interstitial nephritis","Polycystic kidney disease"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Valproate.","Correct. Also angle-closure glaucoma.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Which anticonvulsant causes weight loss and paresthesias?","o":["Lamotrigine","Gabapentin","Topiramate","Valproate","Phenytoin"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Weight gain.","Correct.","Wrong. Weight gain.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"A patient on topiramate develops sudden eye pain, halos and blurry vision. What has occurred?","o":["Optic neuritis","Uveitis","Cataract","Retinal detachment","Angle-closure glaucoma"],"e":["Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Phenytoin can cause which bone and nutritional effects?","o":["Vitamin A toxicity","Iron overload","Osteopetrosis and vitamin K excess","Osteoporosis and folate depletion","Hypercalcemia"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Which drugs are GABA-A agonists used for seizures?","o":["Lamotrigine","Ethosuximide and gabapentin","Phenytoin and carbamazepine","Levetiracetam","Benzodiazepines, phenobarbital and topiramate"],"e":["Wrong. Na+ channel blocker.","Wrong. Ca2+ channel blockers.","Wrong. Na+ channel blockers.","Wrong. SV2A.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"561","k":"Anticonvulsants"},{"s":"Which anticonvulsants are broad spectrum (focal and generalized seizures)?","o":["Phenytoin and carbamazepine","Phenobarbital only","Ethosuximide only","Valproate, lamotrigine, levetiracetam and topiramate","Gabapentin only"],"e":["Wrong. Narrow spectrum.","Wrong. Not the listed group.","Wrong. Absence only.","Correct.","Wrong. Focal."],"a":3,"p":"560","k":"Anticonvulsants"}]},{"t":"Barbiturates, benzodiazepines, sleep drugs, and triptans","q":[{"s":"Barbiturates facilitate GABA-A action by:","o":["Blocking Na+ channels","Increasing the frequency of Cl− channel opening","Blocking GABA reuptake","Increasing the duration of Cl− channel opening","Inhibiting GABA transaminase"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Benzodiazepines.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. \"Barbidurates.\"","Wrong. Valproate."],"a":3,"p":"561","k":"Barbiturates"},{"s":"Benzodiazepines facilitate GABA-A action by:","o":["Activating GABA-B receptors","Directly opening Cl− channels at all doses","Blocking NMDA receptors","Increasing the duration of Cl− channel opening","Increasing the frequency of Cl− channel opening"],"e":["Wrong. Baclofen.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Barbiturates.","Correct. \"Frenzodiazepines.\""],"a":4,"p":"562","k":"Benzodiazepines"},{"s":"A patient with acute intermittent porphyria should not receive which sedative?","o":["Phenobarbital","Zolpidem","Lorazepam","Propofol","Ramelteon"],"e":["Correct. Barbiturates are contraindicated in porphyria.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not the listed contraindication.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not listed."],"a":0,"p":"561","k":"Barbiturates"},{"s":"Compared with benzodiazepines, barbiturates have:","o":["No CYP450 effects","Less risk of respiratory depression","No dependence","A specific antidote","Greater risk of respiratory and cardiovascular depression, which can be fatal"],"e":["Wrong. Induce CYP450.","Wrong. More risk.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Supportive care only.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"561","k":"Barbiturates"},{"s":"How is barbiturate overdose treated?","o":["Physostigmine","Supportive care: assist respiration and maintain BP","Flumazenil","Naloxone","Dantrolene"],"e":["Wrong. Anticholinergics.","Correct.","Wrong. Benzodiazepines.","Wrong. Opioids.","Wrong. Malignant hyperthermia."],"a":1,"p":"561","k":"Barbiturates"},{"s":"Which benzodiazepines are short acting and have higher addictive potential?","o":["Diazepam and chlordiazepoxide","Lorazepam and temazepam only","All benzodiazepines equally","Alprazolam, triazolam, oxazepam and midazolam","Clonazepam only"],"e":["Wrong. Long acting.","Wrong. Not the mnemonic.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. \"ATOM.\"","Wrong. Not listed."],"a":3,"p":"562","k":"Benzodiazepines"},{"s":"A patient with cirrhosis is admitted for alcohol withdrawal. Which benzodiazepine is safest?","o":["Alprazolam","Chlordiazepoxide","Lorazepam","Midazolam","Diazepam"],"e":["Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Hepatic metabolism.","Correct. Also oxazepam and temazepam (\"LOT\").","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Hepatic metabolism, active metabolites."],"a":2,"p":"562","k":"Benzodiazepines"},{"s":"A woman has been seizing for 7 minutes. Wha