rong. Ramelteon.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"562","k":"Insomnia therapy"},{"s":"Suvorexant is contraindicated in which condition?","o":["Hypertension","Depression","Diabetes","Narcolepsy","Asthma"],"e":["Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not listed.","Correct. Also with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors.","Wrong. Not listed."],"a":3,"p":"562","k":"Insomnia therapy"},{"s":"An older adult wants a sleep aid with no known risk of dependency. Which drug fits?","o":["Phenobarbital","Triazolam","Ramelteon","Zolpidem","Alprazolam"],"e":["Wrong. Dependence.","Wrong. High dependence.","Correct. MT1/MT2 agonist in the suprachiasmatic nucleus.","Wrong. Some dependency risk.","Wrong. Dependence."],"a":2,"p":"562","k":"Insomnia therapy"},{"s":"Sumatriptan acts by:","o":["Blocking 5-HT3 receptors","Agonizing 5-HT1B/1D receptors","Blocking D2 receptors","Blocking CGRP receptors","Inhibiting serotonin reuptake"],"e":["Wrong. Ondansetron.","Correct. Inhibits trigeminal activation and causes vasoconstriction.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"562","k":"Triptans"},{"s":"A 58-year-old man with stable angina asks for migraine treatment. Why should sumatriptan be avoided?","o":["It can cause coronary vasospasm","It causes bradycardia","It causes hepatotoxicity","It causes hypotension","It causes bronchospasm"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"562","k":"Triptans"},{"s":"Combining sumatriptan with an SSRI risks:","o":["Hypertensive crisis","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Malignant hyperthermia","Serotonin syndrome","Respiratory depression"],"e":["Wrong. MAOI + tyramine.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"562","k":"Triptans"},{"s":"Long-acting benzodiazepines preferred for alcohol withdrawal include:","o":["Alprazolam","Ramelteon","Triazolam and midazolam","Zolpidem","Chlordiazepoxide and diazepam"],"e":["Wrong. Short acting.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Short acting.","Wrong. Not a benzodiazepine.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"562","k":"Benzodiazepines"},{"s":"Barbiturate CNS depression is exacerbated by:","o":["Caffeine","Vitamin B6","Alcohol use","Folate","Flumazenil"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Additive depression.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"561","k":"Barbiturates"}]},{"t":"Parkinson disease and neurodegenerative disease therapy","q":[{"s":"Why is carbidopa given with levodopa?","o":["To block MAO-B","To inhibit central COMT","To activate dopamine receptors directly","To increase dopamine release","To inhibit peripheral DOPA decarboxylase, increasing brain L-DOPA and reducing nausea"],"e":["Wrong. Selegiline.","Wrong. Tolcapone.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Amantadine.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"563","k":"Parkinson disease therapy"},{"s":"Unlike dopamine, L-DOPA:","o":["Acts directly on D2 receptors","Is an MAO-B inhibitor","Cannot cross the blood-brain barrier","Crosses the blood-brain barrier and is converted to dopamine in the CNS","Is not metabolized"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Reversed.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"564","k":"Carbidopa/levodopa"},{"s":"A patient with long-standing Parkinson disease has periods of good mobility alternating with sudden immobility as each dose wears off. This is:","o":["Serotonin syndrome","Tardive dyskinesia","Akathisia","The \"on-off\" phenomenon","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"564","k":"Carbidopa/levodopa"},{"s":"Which drug blocks peripheral COMT to prevent L-DOPA conversion to 3-O-methyldopa but does not cross the BBB?","o":["Tolcapone","Selegiline","Entacapone","Carbidopa","Amantadine"],"e":["Wrong. Crosses the BBB.","Wrong. MAO-B.","Correct.","Wrong. DOPA decarboxylase.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"563","k":"Parkinson disease therapy"},{"s":"Which COMT inhibitor also acts centrally?","o":["Rasagiline","Entacapone","Tolcapone","Pramipexole","Benztropine"],"e":["Wrong. MAO-B.","Wrong. Peripheral only.","Correct. \"Tolcapone pays the toll and can cross the BBB.\"","Wrong. Agonist.","Wrong. Antimuscarinic."],"a":2,"p":"563","k":"Parkinson disease therapy"},{"s":"Selegiline and rasagiline act by:","o":["Selectively inhibiting MAO-B, preventing dopamine breakdown to DOPAC","Agonizing dopamine receptors","Inhibiting DOPA decarboxylase","Blocking muscarinic receptors","Inhibiting COMT"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Pramipexole.","Wrong. Carbidopa.","Wrong. Benztropine.","Wrong. Entacapone, tolcapone."],"a":0,"p":"563","k":"Parkinson disease therapy"},{"s":"A 55-year-old on pramipexole for Parkinson disease begins gambling compulsively. What drug class causes this?","o":["NMDA antagonists","Non-ergot dopamine agonists","COMT inhibitors","MAO-B inhibitors","Antimuscarinics"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Impulse control disorder.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not the listed effect.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"563","k":"Parkinson disease therapy"},{"s":"A patient on levodopa develops troublesome dyskinesias. Which drug reduces them and may cause livedo reticularis?","o":["Selegiline","Entacapone","Benztropine","Amantadine","Bromocriptine"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Increases dopamine release and decreases reuptake.","Wrong. Ergot."],"a":3,"p":"563","k":"Parkinson disease therapy"},{"s":"Benztropine is most effective for which Parkinson symptoms?","o":["Tremor and rigidity","Postural instability","Bradykinesia","Hallucinations","Dementia"],"e":["Correct. Little effect on bradykinesia.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. May worsen."],"a":0,"p":"563","k":"Parkinson disease therapy"},{"s":"Which dopamine agonist is an ergot deri